WEBVTT

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.332
A grammar guarantees it parses.

00:00:03.332 --> 00:00:04.934
Not that it's right.

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:08.138
Strict tool calling, read against XGrammar's own benchmark.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.614
Llama-3.2-1B on BFCL-V3: schema accuracy.

00:00:12.614 --> 00:00:18.318
Without the tag, 21%; with the tag, 100%.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.119
The 79 points of unparseable output, once constrained.

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:25.983
41 of 79: became correct output.

00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:29.801
38 of 79: parse cleanly and are wrong.

00:00:29.801 --> 00:00:33.142
Approximate: read off the post's bar charts.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:41.725
82.4x compilation speedup at 500 tools: 60.3 seconds to 0.73.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.270
A grammar's guarantee is a proof, and worth exactly nothing about correctness.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:54.608
Every number here is from the full article, with its sources, at ai.thesatyajit.com.
